# Sass Architecture

## Directory shape

```
src/sass/
├── index.scss                  # entry — @use order matters
├── _tools.scss                 # @forward of tokens/mixins — no CSS
├── _settings.scss              # breakpoints, radii, non-theme.json values
├── _theme-json-variable.scss   # theme.json → Sass token bridge
├── _mixins.scss                # global mixins
├── _wp-default-overrides.scss  # core block resets
└── {capability}/               # one directory per capability
    ├── _index.scss             # required — @use's the directory's partials
    └── _{component}.scss
```

Every capability directory carries an `_index.scss` and is `@use`'d once from
`src/sass/index.scss` (as `@use "woocommerce"`, never `@use "woocommerce/index"`
— two `*/index` paths collide on the `index` namespace). **Name directories
after the capability**, matching the PHP convention — a project without that
capability deletes the directory and its one `@use` line.

Admin styles live in `src/sass-admin/`, entered from `src/index.js`.

## Module system

This tree is Dart Sass modules. **Never `@import`.** `@use` does not leak
members, so every sheet that needs tokens or mixins loads them itself:

```scss
@use "../tools" as *;                 // from src/sass/{capability}/
@use "../../src/sass/tools" as *;     // from src-jsx-blocks/{block}/
```

`_tools.scss` `@forward`s settings, mixins, icon-font tokens/mixins, form
tokens/mixins, tag mixins, and menu tokens/mixins. **Do not `@forward`
CSS-emitting partials from tools** — every `@use "tools"` would then dump
that CSS at the root of the shop, the editor, and each JSX block.

Header shell files use `src/sass/header/_tools.scss` instead. It hides the
four header measurements in settings so `header/_variables.scss` (rem) can
win. Do not `@use` both `../tools` and `header/variables` as `*`.

Editor canvas: `@use` cannot nest. `src/sass-admin/index.scss` injects
frontend sheets with `meta.load-css()` inside `.editor-styles-wrapper`.
Adding a capability means a `@use` in `src/sass/index.scss` **and** a
`meta.load-css()` line there.

Built-in functions live in modules: `@use "sass:map"` then `map.get` /
`map.has-key` / `map.keys`. Never global `map-get`. `lighten()` is
`color.adjust(..., $lightness: ...)`.

Never put `//` on the same line as a Sass map value
(`wide: 1480px, // ...`). stylelint `--fix` will eat the key. Comments
belong above the map. Do not `--fix` `icon-font/_variables.scss` — literal
PUA glyphs.

## Token tiers — never skip a tier

**Tier 1** — `_theme-json-variable.scss` maps `theme.json` presets to Sass
variables. This is the only file that writes `var(--wp--...)`:

```scss
$color-primary: var(--wp--preset--color--primary);
$spacing-large: var(--wp--preset--spacing--large);
$grid-container: var(--wp--style--global--content-size);
```

**Tier 2** — a capability's `_variables.scss` defines semantic tokens that
*reference* Tier 1. Never paste a raw hex or px value from a design file here
without mapping it through a Tier 1 token:

```scss
// menu/_variables.scss
$menu-nav-link-hover-color: $color-primary;
```

**Tier 3** — component partials consume Tier 2 only. A component partial should
not reach past its capability's variables to `$color-primary` directly, and must
never write `var(--wp--preset--...)` inline.

## Breakpoints

```scss
// navigation: header → mobile drawer. medium: WP columns wrap.
// wide: matches theme.json settings.layout.wideSize.
$breakpoints: (
	small: 0,
	wp-small: 600px,
	navigation: 767px,
	medium: 781px,
	uagb-medium: 767px,
	large: 1024px,
	wide: 1480px,
	hd: 1920px,
	unlimited: 9999px
);
```

Mobile-first, via the mixin — never a raw `@media` query:

```scss
.component {
	padding: 1rem;

	@include breakpoint(medium) { padding: 1.5rem; }
	@include breakpoint(large)  { padding: 2rem; }
}
```

A capability needing its own desktop/mobile split defines **one** breakpoint
variable plus wrapper mixins in its own `_mixins.scss`, so the switch point moves
from a single place. See `.cursor/rules/sf-menu-architecture.mdc`.

## The `theme.json` TODO method

`_settings.scss` carries deliberate TODO markers where a Sass value duplicates
something `theme.json` owns:

```scss
// wide: matches theme.json settings.layout.wideSize.
$radius-btn: 0; // matches theme.json styles.elements.button.border.radius
```

These are **verification checkpoints, not decoration**. When you touch a file
containing one, resolve it: read the corresponding `theme.json` value
(`settings.layout.wideSize`, `settings.color.palette`,
`settings.spacing.spacingSizes`, `settings.typography.fontSizes`) and either
align the Sass value or replace it with the Tier 1 token. Put the note
**above** a map, never on the value line.

Do not delete a TODO without aligning the values, and do not assume a TODO has
already been resolved — at least one in this theme has been open long enough for
the two values to diverge. Check `PROJECT.md` § Known debt.

## Conventions

- BEM for class names, kebab-case for files and directories
- Partials are `_name.scss`; directory entry points are `_index.scss`
- Keep selectors flat; avoid deep nesting
- Prefix custom classes with the project prefix (`hw-`) so third-party plugin CSS
  cannot collide. Generic names like `.has-icon` or `.mobile-menu` have collided
  with plugin and commercial-theme styles before.
- Pair `:hover` with `:focus-visible` on anything interactive. Never remove an
  outline without providing a replacement indicator.

## Styling markup you don't control

For plugin or WooCommerce template output where no class can be injected, target
the plugin's own selector and pull in what you need via a mixin rather than
asking for markup changes:

```scss
.add_to_cart_button {
	@include hw-icon-button( 'shopping-cart' );
}
```

